{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.Hecke.CrossingReversalParity","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.Hecke.CrossingReversalParity","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.Ring.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.NoncommRing","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.Abel","lean:module:Mathlib.Analysis.SpecialFunctions.Pow.Real","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.Positivity","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.FieldSimp"],"declarations":19,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.CrossingReversalParity.splits_sum_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.CrossingReversalParity.splits_sum_zero","kind":"theorem","line":447,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/CrossingReversalParity.lean#L447-L451","doc":"The two are exact negatives, so the channel choice is the whole content: the\nmagnitude is settled and only the sign is at stake. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.CrossingReversalParity.symmetricSplit_pos","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.CrossingReversalParity.symmetricSplit_pos","kind":"theorem","line":438,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/CrossingReversalParity.lean#L438-L445","doc":"And the symmetric channel gives it with the right sign. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.CrossingReversalParity.alternatingSplit_neg","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.CrossingReversalParity.alternatingSplit_neg","kind":"theorem","line":422,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/CrossingReversalParity.lean#L422-L436","doc":"**The alternating channel gives the split negative**: `alternatingSplit q < 0`\nfor every `1 < q`, i.e. a neutron *lighter* than the proton, where\n`δ = (m_n − m_p)/M_N` is positive.  With `symmetricSplit_pos` this is what makes\n`splits_sum_zero` bite: the two one-dimensional channels cancel under the\ncorpus's equal weights `w_sym = w_alt`.  Conditional on the identification\ndescribed in the section header, which is not the block's. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.CrossingReversalParity.alternatingSplit","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.CrossingReversalParity.alternatingSplit","kind":"def","line":415,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/CrossingReversalParity.lean#L415-L420","doc":"The split built from the **alternating** (sign) one-dimensional channel\n`σ ↦ −q⁻¹`.  (Earlier docstring: \"the one the conjecture's own left-handedness\nargument selects\" — retracted, see the file header; the block selects that\nchannel for its *residual*, not for the split.) ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.CrossingReversalParity.symmetricSplit","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.CrossingReversalParity.symmetricSplit","kind":"def","line":412,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/CrossingReversalParity.lean#L412-L413","doc":"The split built from the symmetric channel. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.CrossingReversalParity.flavourSplit_eq_hbarSq_symmetric_div_three","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.CrossingReversalParity.flavourSplit_eq_hbarSq_symmetric_div_three","kind":"theorem","line":404,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/CrossingReversalParity.lean#L404-L410","doc":"**The flavour split is the symmetric-channel value**, exactly:\n`δ = ℏ_q²·(q² − q⁻²)/3` with `ℏ_q = 1 − q⁻¹`.  Same identity the probe checks\nsymbolically; here so the sign statement below has something to be about. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.CrossingReversalParity.symmetricValue_pos","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.CrossingReversalParity.symmetricValue_pos","kind":"theorem","line":395,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/CrossingReversalParity.lean#L395-L402","doc":"On the substrate branch `1 < q` the symmetric value is positive. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.CrossingReversalParity.alternating_eq_neg_symmetric","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.CrossingReversalParity.alternating_eq_neg_symmetric","kind":"theorem","line":390,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/CrossingReversalParity.lean#L390-L393","doc":"The two channels differ by a sign and nothing else: same magnitude. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.CrossingReversalParity.alternatingValue","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.CrossingReversalParity.alternatingValue","kind":"def","line":386,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/CrossingReversalParity.lean#L386-L388","doc":"The `k = 2` reversal evaluated on the **alternating** (sign) eigenvalue\n`σ ↦ −q⁻¹` — the channel `conj:flavor-split-habiro-derivation` selects. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.CrossingReversalParity.symmetricValue","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.CrossingReversalParity.symmetricValue","kind":"def","line":383,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/CrossingReversalParity.lean#L383-L384","doc":"The `k = 2` reversal evaluated on the **symmetric** eigenvalue `σ ↦ q`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.CrossingReversalParity.reversal_rec","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.CrossingReversalParity.reversal_rec","kind":"theorem","line":314,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/CrossingReversalParity.lean#L314-L344","doc":"`D_{k+2} = u·D_{k+1} − D_k` with `u = σ + σ⁻¹`.  Together with\n`resolvent_sq` (`u² = h² + 4` central) this is the general mechanism: `D_k` is `h`\ntimes a Chebyshev polynomial in `u` of degree `k−1`, carrying only powers of `u`\ncongruent to `k−1` mod 2.  The general-`k` conclusion is **not** formalised here —\nsee the probe named in the header. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.CrossingReversalParity.reversal_five","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.CrossingReversalParity.reversal_five","kind":"theorem","line":265,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/CrossingReversalParity.lean#L265-L310","doc":"**`k = 5` IS central**, at `h(h⁴ + 5h² + 5)` — the odd case one step past the\n`k = 3` result the corpus already carries, which is what shows `k = 3`'s\ncentrality is a parity rather than a coincidence. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.CrossingReversalParity.reversal_four","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.CrossingReversalParity.reversal_four","kind":"theorem","line":234,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/CrossingReversalParity.lean#L234-L261","doc":"**`k = 4` has the same shape** — `h(h²+2)·u`, still carrying `σ`.  So the\nfailure at `k = 2` is parity, not a small-exponent accident. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.CrossingReversalParity.reversal_two_forces_resolvent_central","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.CrossingReversalParity.reversal_two_forces_resolvent_central","kind":"theorem","line":218,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/CrossingReversalParity.lean#L218-L232","doc":"**The obstruction.**  If the `k = 2` reversal equals an element `z`, then\n`h·u = z`.  So when `h` is invertible, a *central* value at `k = 2` forces the\nresolvent `u = σ + σ⁻¹` — hence `σ` itself — to be central.  In `H_n(q)` with\n`n ≥ 3` the generators do not commute, so `σ` is not central and the square\nreversal is not a scalar.  This is what the `k = 2` route into `δ` has to get\npast, and it is the step `conj:flavor-split-habiro-derivation` does not supply. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.CrossingReversalParity.reversal_two","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.CrossingReversalParity.reversal_two","kind":"theorem","line":211,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/CrossingReversalParity.lean#L211-L216","doc":"**`k = 2` is NOT central**: the square reversal is `h·u`, and `u = σ + σ⁻¹`\ncarries `σ`.  Contrast `σ³ − σ⁻³ = h(h² + 3)`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.CrossingReversalParity.resolvent_sq","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.CrossingReversalParity.resolvent_sq","kind":"theorem","line":188,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/CrossingReversalParity.lean#L188-L207","doc":"**The invariant.**  The resolvent `u = σ + σ⁻¹` squares to the central scalar\n`h² + 4`.  This is what makes the parity work: every *even* power of `u` is\ncentral, every odd one carries `σ`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.CrossingReversalParity.inv_quad","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.CrossingReversalParity.inv_quad","kind":"theorem","line":176,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/CrossingReversalParity.lean#L176-L186","doc":"The **conjugate quadratic**: the mirror generator satisfies `σ⁻² = 1 - hσ⁻¹`.\nThis is what lets every power of `σ⁻¹` be reduced without any binomial expansion,\nand it is where the alternating sign that produces the parity comes from. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.CrossingReversalParity.sub_eq","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.CrossingReversalParity.sub_eq","kind":"theorem","line":171,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/CrossingReversalParity.lean#L171-L174","doc":"`σ - σ⁻¹ = h`: the odd cases collapse through this. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.CrossingReversalParity.inv_eq","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.CrossingReversalParity.inv_eq","kind":"theorem","line":156,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/CrossingReversalParity.lean#L156-L169","doc":"The mirror generator is `σ - h`: the quadratic upgrades a **left** inverse to\nan explicit formula.  Same argument as\n`QOU.FlavorSplit.topological_flavor_split_of_central`, reproved locally because\nthat declaration lives in a content-block sibling rather than a library module and\nso cannot be imported. ","signatureChunk":0}]}